\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{Multiplicity spaces, squares and the grading}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{On an even-dimensional family of directions both
\vocabk{charge conjugation signs}{fdip-017H} exist at all
multiplicities. On an odd family #{n=2m+1}, the sign
#{\eta=(-1)^m} exists at all multiplicities, and the opposite
sign exists precisely when #{\dim E_+=\dim E_-} in
\ref{fdip-017N}. For a branch preserving the simple types,
write #{\tau=\pm1} for the square sign on a simple. A prescribed
scalar square #{\tau'\bbI}, #{\tau'=\pm1}, is possible
exactly when each multiplicity with #{\tau'\ne\tau} is even.
On equally many exchanged copies either scalar square is possible.}

\p{In even dimension #{n=2m} either sign conjugates the
canonical grading #{C=\ui^m\psI} of \ref{fdip-009X} to
#{(-1)^m C}. For an independently supplied
\vocabk{grading}{fdip-013N} #{\Gamma}, preserving or exchanging
its two eigenspaces requires #{c_\eta\Gamma c_\eta^{-1}=\Gamma}
or #{-\Gamma}, respectively.}

\proof{
\p{Conjugating #{C_{2m}\varepsilon_{2m+1}} gives the factor
#{(-1)^m\eta} by \ref{fdip-017O}. Consequently the odd
multiplicity spaces of opposite signs must have equal dimensions
when the factor is negative. In that case choose the same
orthonormal multiplicity coordinates on both summands. Apply
the opposite-sign even-model antiunitary of \ref{fdip-017O} to the
simple factor and coordinate conjugation to the multiplicity
factor, obtaining an antiunitary #{b} of square #{\tau_b\bbI},
#{\tau_b=\pm1}. At #{m=0} use ordinary conjugation. On the
identified copies take
#{(v,w)\mapsto(bw,\,\tau'\tau_b b v)}.
The swap supplies the extra sign on the last direction and the
square is #{\tau'\bbI}.}

\p{On a preserved type, tensor a fixed simple antiunitary with
coordinate conjugation on #{E}. This is well defined because
moving a scalar between factors conjugates it on either one.
Any other antiunitary of that sign differs by a unitary
commuting with all directions. The matrix-unit argument of \ref{fdip-017N}
identifies that unitary with a unitary #{u} on #{E} alone.
Thus the square on this summand is
#{\tau\bbI_{S_0}\otimes(u\bar u)} in the chosen coordinates.
One can always take #{u=\bbI}. To obtain #{u\bar u=-\bbI_E},
determinants give #{1=|\det u|^2=(-1)^{\dim E}}. Conversely,
when #{\dim E} is even, pair an orthonormal basis and take
the real signed exchange on each pair, sending its first member
to its second and its second to minus its first. This gives
the required negative square. Choices on preserved types are independent.
On a nonzero space a scalar square of an antiunitary is real, since the antiunitary
commutes with its own square and conjugates its scalar.
Unitarity then restricts that scalar to #{\pm1}.}

\p{In the even volume product all #{2m} generator signs cancel,
and conjugating its phase gives #{(-1)^m} as above. For
#{\Gamma} with real eigenvalues #{\pm1}, the indicated identities
are equivalent to carrying each sign space to the corresponding
same or opposite space.}
}
